WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.504
A card is declined, and authorize throws.

2
00:00:05.504 --> 00:00:08.239
Nothing in its Kotlin signature says so.

3
00:00:08.239 --> 00:00:12.341
A new endpoint forgets the try, and the customer gets a 500.

4
00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:14.451
A decline happens every day.

5
00:00:14.451 --> 00:00:19.364
Return it as a value: a result that holds either the value, or the error.

6
00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:23.131
Chain the steps of a payment: validate, authorise, capture.

7
00:00:23.131 --> 00:00:28.327
Each step keeps the payment on the success track, or switches it to the failure track.

8
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.755
A request for 500 cents passes all three steps, and comes out as Ok.

9
00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:36.412
Now a request for 50,000 cents.

10
00:00:36.412 --> 00:00:39.136
The issuer declines it at authorise.

11
00:00:39.136 --> 00:00:40.423
Does capture run?

12
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:43.033
No.

13
00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:48.024
The train switches to the failure track, and rides past capture to the end.

14
00:00:48.024 --> 00:00:51.351
The first failure wins, and nothing after it runs.

15
00:00:51.351 --> 00:00:53.015
That is called fail fast.

16
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:58.467
Fail fast suits a pipeline, where each step needs the one before.

17
00:00:58.467 --> 00:00:59.240
Not a form.

18
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.456
A client sends an amount of minus 5 and an unknown currency.

19
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:05.612
Fail fast stops at the amount.

20
00:01:05.612 --> 00:01:12.811
The currency check never runs, so the client fixes one field, sends again, and only then hears about the other.

21
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:17.589
To collect every error, run the independent checks side by side.

22
00:01:17.589 --> 00:01:20.515
Both fail, and both errors land in one list.

23
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:27.555
In Arrow, zipOrAccumulate does this: every check runs, and the Amount is built only if all of them pass.

24
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:30.186
One trap sits at the edge.

25
00:01:30.186 --> 00:01:35.543
runCatching catches every Throwable, and in a coroutine that includes the cancel.

26
00:01:35.543 --> 00:01:37.594
Cancel this job while it waits.

27
00:01:37.594 --> 00:01:43.149
The cancel becomes a failed Result, the job runs on, and the card is charged anyway.

28
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:48.174
Rethrow CancellationException in onFailure, and the cancel keeps going.

29
00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:49.479
Nothing is charged.

30
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.931
In code, Arrow's either block fails fast, and zipOrAccumulate collects.

31
00:01:54.931 --> 00:01:58.911
Java has no Arrow: you collect the errors in a list yourself.

32
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.665
So: fail fast when each step needs the one before.

33
00:02:03.665 --> 00:02:08.351
Collect every error when the checks are independent, like the fields of a form.

34
00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:09.543
A quick check.

35
00:02:09.543 --> 00:02:12.752
A request for minus 1 cents rides the railway.

36
00:02:12.752 --> 00:02:15.264
Which steps run, and what comes out?

37
00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:18.639
Only validate.

38
00:02:18.639 --> 00:02:22.181
It fails, and the result is an Err with the reason.
